{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And Pharaoh called Abram.</b> It is plausible that when the plagues came suddenly upon him and his household at the time Sarah was taken into his house, he thought to himself, saying, \"What is this that Elohim has done to us?\" and he asked her and she told him that she was his wife, and therefore he called Abram and accused him. Or as our Sages said (Genesis Rabbah 41:2), that he was stricken with a disease that made intercourse difficult for him, and he suspected that perhaps she was a wife, and he said to him tentatively, \"What is this you have done to me,\" in order to extract the truth from his mouth, for if she were his sister he would have said, \"Indeed, she is my sister.\" And he said, \"And now, here is your wife, take her and go,\" in order to see what he would say and how he would respond to his rebuke, but Abram was silent and answered him nothing out of his great fear. Then Pharaoh understood that she was his wife, just as he had suspected, and Pharaoh appointed men over them to escort them away."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The abbreviation ב\"ר is resolved as בראשית רבה (Genesis Rabbah); the citation מא ב follows the standard chapter:verse convention."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:27:54.177128141Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}